# FY / year-end P&L: two valid Nets (learn before “fixing” −$927)

## Invariant

**Net = Income − Expenses** is always true.  
What changes is whether Income/Expenses are **GST-inclusive (bank cash)** or **GST-exclusive (tax / CTR style)**.

| Basis | Income (SELPIC FY fixture) | Expenses | Net |
|-------|----------------------------|----------|-----|
| **Real-Time / Business Summary (cash)** | ~$14,419.48 incl. | $15,346.61 incl. | **−$927.13** |
| **Tax / accountant (ex GST)** | ≈ $14,419 − 1A ≈ **$13,108.62** | ≈ $15,347 − 1B ≈ **$14,783** | **≈ −$1,674** |

Bridge: `Net_exGST ≈ Net_inclusive − (1A − 1B)`  
(e.g. −927 − ~747 ≈ −1,674).

Quarters use the **same** engine. FY does not switch formula — the GST gap is just larger and more visible vs the accountant pack.

## GST-FREE purchases (already correct in engine)

`lib/gst/purchase-gst-claimable.ts`:

- Explicit **FREE** / **EXCLUDED** / manual default → **not** in `taxableExpenses` → **not** in 1B (no ÷11).
- Face value **still** in `totalExpenses` (P&L cash cost).
- Only **INCLUDED** / default bank claimable lines → ÷11 for 1B.

So ex-GST expenses are **not** “total expenses × 10/11”. They are:

`Expenses_exGST ≈ totalExpenses − 1B`  
(= claimable lines stripped of GST + GST-free/non-claimable at full face value).

## What each screen is for

| UI | Basis today | Use for |
|----|-------------|---------|
| Real-Time P&L, Business Summary Income/Expenses/Net | Inclusive cash | Bank reconciliation |
| GST Summary 1A / 1B / Net | ÷11 estimates | **BAS** (quarterly) |
| Taxable Income card (−$927 on FY) | Same as inclusive Net today | **Not** automatic CTR “excluding GST” profit |
| ATO Lodgment BAS | Same metrics / 1A·1B | OK for BAS estimates |
| ATO Lodgment **Annual / CTR** | **Tax (ex GST)** via `totalIncomeExGst` / `totalExpensesExGst` / `netProfitExGst` | Copy into myTax / company return — **not** cash Net |
| Annual P&L check card | Tax primary + cash footnote | Prevents pasting Biz Intel −$927 as CTR profit |
| **Balance Sheet** Equity | CTR primary (−$1,674); cash secondary (−$927); bridge 1A−1B (~+$747); **Total RE (CTR / ex GST when GST Payable on BS)** | CTR ≠ Biz Intel; RE keeps BS balanced |
| **Balance Sheet** GST Payable | **Latest BAS quarter due** (e.g. Q4 ~$765); ATO refund is **Cash**, not a payable reduction | See `accounting-balance-sheet-gst-profit.mdc` |

## Balance Sheet equity (learned)

```
CTR Net (−1,674.16) + Net GST 1A−1B (+747.03) = Cash Net (−927.13)
Total RE (CTR / ex GST when GST Payable on BS) = Opening RE + Cash Net
Total Equity = Opening Capital + Share Capital + Total RE (CTR / ex GST when GST Payable on BS)
```

**Wrong:** Equity primary profit = cash −$927 as if CTR.  
**Right:** Primary = `netProfitExGst`; Total RE stays cash; show GST bridge between them.

Helpers: `lib/gst/outstanding-bas-gst.ts`, `computeBalanceSheet` in `lib/utils/balance-sheet.ts`.

## Product direction (implemented)

For selected P&L period (FY and quarters):

1. Business Summary / Real-Time P&L show Income **incl. + ex GST (est.)**  
2. Expenses show **incl. + ex GST (est.)** with FREE lines at face (`totalExpenses − 1B`)  
3. Net shows **cash** and **tax (est.)** side by side  
4. Tax Provision primary Taxable Income = **tax (ex GST)**; cash P&L shown as secondary  
5. **ATO Annual + Company CTR** primary totals = **tax (ex GST)**; cash shown only as reconciliation  
6. Do **not** replace bank P&L with tax P&L silently on Biz Intel — cash stays the large figure  

Engine fields: `totalIncomeExGst` / `totalExpensesExGst` / `netProfitExGst` on `calculateBusinessMetrics`.  

## Do not

- Call −$927 a “bug” vs −$1,674 without stating basis  
- Blindly remove 10% from all expenses (breaks GST-FREE)  
- Lodge company income tax using inclusive Net as if it were ex-GST CTR profit  
- Assume quarterly BAS being right means FY Taxable Income card is already tax-basis  

## Confirmed fixture (SELPIC FY 2025–26)

- Q2 exp incl. $1,516.08 · Q3 $7,570.19 · Q4 $6,260.34 → FY $15,346.61  
- 1B FY ≈ $563.83 → exp ex ≈ $14,782.78  
- Income ex ≈ $13,108.62 → tax Net ≈ **−$1,674**
